  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSeveral weak shortwaves under the main upper ridge currently\nbringing some thunderstorms to central ND and eastern SD.\nThe main surface low and inverted trough is still over the\nwestern Dakotas, and should begin to move eastward tomorrow as\nthe flow turns southwesterly and a lead shortwave moves out. The\nmain upper trough starts to move into Saskatchewan and Manitoba\nMonday night, pushing the surface trough and associated cold\nfront east into the Red River Valley by early Tuesday. West to \nnorthwest surface winds will pick up Tuesday and Wednesday \nbehind the surface front with decent cold air advection and\npressure gradient, possibly meeting advisory criteria. Near\nzonal flow sets up late week and into next weekend, although a\ncluster of 34 percent of ensemble members tries to dig a trough\ninto the Northern Plains. There is a signal in the ECMWF EFI for\na break in the above normal temperatures by next weekend.  \n\n...Severe chances this afternoon and evening...\n\nWater vapor loop has the main shortwaves to our south or west,\nbut there is a small vort currently moving through central ND\nthat could provide support for more convection as it moves into\nour forecast area. ML CAPE is around 3000 J/kg, and while deep\nlayer shear is not super strong it is around 30 to 35 kts which\nshould be enough to keep some updrafts going. The low level\nlapse rates are not too impressive given the stratus we have\nseen much of the day, but there is a decent amount of\ninstability thanks to dew points in the 70s and temps in the\n80s. CAMs continue to be very unreliable and enough ensemble\nmembers of the HREF were showing good probability of updraft\nhelicity tracks over the southern Red River Valley this morning,\nwhich have not developed. Some of the machine learning runs show\nsome low to moderate chances for severe impacts, while other\nruns do not. Can't completely rule out more convection\ndeveloping as the central ND vort moves east, but uncertainty is\nhigh as to what actually develops. If severe storms do develop,\nthe main threat will be large hail. \n\n...Marginal risk for severe Monday...\n\nThe ridge axis shifts east tomorrow and the flow aloft becomes\nsouthwesterly. Deep layer shear will be increasing up into the\n40 to 50 kt range by tomorrow afternoon. But the instability\nbecomes more uncertain. Some of the ensemble members don't have\nany surface based CAPE at all, but enough do that the mean CAPE\ninto eastern ND is around 1500 J/kg. Much will depend on what\nexactly happens this evening and into the overnight. HREF and\nREFS updraft helicity probabilities have some chance, but not\nsuper impressive. At this point with all the unknowns the level\n1 risk seems reasonable.",
